BBRplus backport host checks
===========================

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bbrplus/test_backport.py --cc clang

Python 3 and a host C compiler are required. HOSTCC is also supported; cc is
the default. The runner extracts production TCP TSO sizing/dispatch, BBRplus
private state, BDP/quantization, probe/drain and ACK-aggregation helper bodies
into a temporary program. Assertions remain enabled even with NDEBUG supplied.

Checks cover 1,080 combinations of pacing rate/shift, TSO goal, device segment
limit and optional callbacks; legacy fallback, the low-rate one/two-segment
threshold, the 127-segment cache cap, randomized probe-cycle lengths, drain to
BDP, app/receiver-window limits, and ACK-aggregation epoch/window accounting.
Seven memory-limit cases extract the production TCP initialization assignments
and check the unchanged floors/initial values, RAM dependence and 16-MiB caps
with modeled 4-KiB pages. Source guards check the shared helper's GPL export,
miatoll's explicit BBRplus default and the global CUBIC fallback.

Negative control (MUST fail with a C assertion):

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bbrplus/test_backport.py --cc clang --donor-hook

This deliberately disables the TCP callback dispatch omitted by the donor.
The memory-limit negative control must also fail with a C assertion:

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bbrplus/test_backport.py --cc clang --old-memory-limits

It deliberately restores the old 4/6-MiB TCP maximum-buffer caps.

The model uses fake sockets, rates, skbs, random values and TCP helper shims.
Its header budget is modeled, not a real device/network header size. It does
not run packet I/O, locking, loss recovery, timer pacing, the complete BBRplus
state machine, or a module loader. It cannot prove throughput, latency or
fairness. model.c is a template for the runner, not a standalone test binary.

See Documentation/networking/bbrplus-backport.rst for configuration, source
provenance and selected-object/partial-link build results.
